Haakon Studebaker <heptapod@gmail.com> writes:
> > If you're playing unspoiled, one good approach is to wish for the most
> > helpful thing you've seen in any of your games thus far.
> 
> You sure about that? That's like telling some new player that it's 
> perfectly good to genocide rothes after losing numerous weak characters to 
> them.

If they'd really had more trouble with rothes than anything else, then
so be it. Seems mildly unlikely, given that "stand in a hallway so they
can't gang up on you" is an intuitive and highly effective countermeasure
to rothes.

Genociding ants[1] might be sound advice for a player who'd fallen to
them often, but who'd never been deep enough to run afoul of mind
flayers or liches.

"Helpful" OTOH, covers a lot of ground; the trick is to know a helpful
thing when you see it.

> For someone new to the game and who hasn't gotten the hang of nutrition, 
> wish for a =oSD.

Even if you are having trouble with hunger, some top-notch armor or weapon
would likely be better, in that it'd give you easy access to more food,
while also solving most short-term fighting-with-monsters problems. 

> Especially helpful if attempting foodless conduct.

If our hypothetical unspoiled n00b is going for foodless, he's got bigger
problems than deciding how to use his wish. 

> Just don't choke on your food.

Choking on food is remarkably hard to do, barring typos or extreme
recklessness (i.e., eating while satiated or answering "Stop eating?"
in the negative).

